  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the whole app in `src/main.ts`. It passes `typecheck`, but I could not run it or `vite build`, so none of it has been checked in a browser. In particular, the app drives the camera every frame with `app.camera.setPose(..., { cut: true })`. If that call is ignored at runtime, the view would stay static.\n\nThe scene has a glowing star with a halo and bloom, 8 planets with distinct sizes and colours, and orbit lines. Each planet spins and has a procedural noise or band look. Earth and Jupiter each have one moon, and Saturn has a ring. Inner planets orbit faster (visual periods are about 12 s for Mercury, rising with the square root of the real period). A procedural starfield sits behind everything.\n\n- **Focus:** clicking a planet, or `__focusPlanet(name)`, eases the camera (cubic in-out, 1.5 s) to a close view and then follows the planet. It shows an info panel with the name, orbital period in Earth days, and distance in million km. Clicking empty space, pressing Escape, or `__unfocus()` eases back to the overview and hides the panel.\n- **Overview controls:** dragging orbits the camera and scrolling zooms, limited to 50–320 units. Both are ignored while focused or while the camera is moving.\n- **Pause:** the button toggles between \"Pause\" and \"Play\" and freezes both orbits and spin.\n- **Test hooks:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a frozen read-only object with getters for `ready`, `focused`, `paused`, `planets` and `cameraDistanceToFocus`. `__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after the engine reports ready.\n\nPlanet picking uses my own ray test against each planet's position, with a minimum click radius of about 9 px so small planets are still clickable.\n\n",
